### What variant of Codex are you using?

Codex App on Windows

### What feature would you like to see?

I would like Codex App on Windows to expose an officially supported per-process restricted execution profile.
My use case is running narrowly scoped tools such as TypeScript type checking against a local repository that contains important uncommitted work.
I need a child process to have:
- read-only access to explicitly selected repository/worktree paths;
- read-only access to explicitly selected dependency paths, including junction targets;
- write access to exactly one dedicated output/evidence directory;
- OS-enforced denial of all other filesystem writes;
- OS-enforced network denial, including child processes;
- process-tree supervision with a hard timeout;
- reliable stdout/stderr and exit-code capture;
- diagnostics showing which sandbox restrictions were actually applied.
The execution should fail closed if the requested restrictions cannot be enforced. It should not silently fall back to a broader writable workspace.
The current execution interface lets me select parameters such as the command, working directory and sandbox mode, but in the environment I tested I could not restrict a single child process so that the repositories were read-only while one dedicated directory remained writable.
I am not asking Codex to modify global Windows ACLs, firewall rules, user accounts, repository junctions, or other machine-wide settings as a workaround.
This would be useful for safely running compilers, type checkers, linters and narrowly scoped tests against repositories with valuable uncommitted changes.

### Additional information

Environment: Windows, Codex App, local NTFS Git repositories/worktrees.
In my case, the available sandbox profile allowed writes to the working repositories. A read-only review confirmed that some Windows sandbox restrictions were present, but there was no exposed interface that could guarantee all of the following together for one child process:
1. repositories read-only;
2. one exclusive writable output directory;
3. network denied;
4. descendants restricted the same way;
5. process-tree timeout/termination;
6. verifiable effective restrictions.
I deliberately did not run the compiler because I could not first establish those confinement guarantees.
This is a feature request / capability gap, not a reported sandbox escape or security vulnerability.
If there is already a supported way to configure this in Codex App, documentation or an example would also solve my use case.
